select m.id, 4, $L$Components & Helpers$L$, $L$17 minute read$L$, $L$Not everything is a page. Modern apps are built from components — a nav bar, a date picker, a data table — that appear on many pages. Component objects and shared helpers let you model those once, but the same restraint applies: reuse what genuinely repeats, and resist abstracting what does not.$L$, array[$L$Model a reusable UI component as a component object scoped to its root$L$, $L$Write small shared helpers for genuinely repeated logic$L$, $L$Apply the rule of three to avoid premature abstraction$L$], $L$## Pages are not the only unit
A page object assumes the meaningful unit is a whole page. Often it is not. A cookie banner, a top navigation bar, a date picker or a results table shows up on many pages and behaves the same everywhere. Modelling each of those inside every page object duplicates them; modelling them once as a **component object** does not.

## Component objects, scoped to a root
A component object is like a page object but scoped to a **root locator** rather than the whole page. You pass it the element it lives inside, and all its locators resolve within that root. This means the same component works wherever it appears, and two instances on one page do not collide:

- The object takes a root `Locator` in its constructor.
- Every internal locator is `this.root.getByRole(...)`, not `page.getByRole(...)`.
- The test creates it by pointing at the specific instance it cares about.

This scoping is what makes components genuinely reusable — and it neatly sidesteps the strict-mode collisions you would get if two identical widgets both matched a page-level locator.

## Helpers for logic, not for wrapping one line
A helper is a plain function for repeated *logic* that is not tied to a page: formatting a test date, generating a unique email, seeding data through the API, waiting on a custom condition. Good helpers are small, pure where possible, and named for intent.

The trap is wrapping a single Playwright call in a helper — `clickButton(page, name)` around `getByRole('button', { name }).click()` — which adds a layer and hides the very API everyone already knows. Helpers should encapsulate something *worth* encapsulating, not rename the framework.

## The rule of three
A practical guard against premature abstraction: **do not abstract until the third occurrence.** The first time you write something, write it inline. The second time, notice the duplication but tolerate it. The third time, extract — because now you have three real examples showing you what actually varies and what stays constant. Abstracting after one or two examples usually produces the wrong abstraction, which is more expensive to unpick than the duplication would have been.

## Key takeaway
Model genuinely repeated UI as component objects scoped to a root locator, and repeated logic as small well-named helpers — but abstract on the third occurrence, not the first, so the shape you extract is informed by real usage rather than a guess.$L$, $L$A component object for a navigation bar, scoped to its root so it works on any page and never collides with anything else. Then a small, honest helper:

~~~ts
// components/nav-bar.component.ts
import { Locator, Page } from '@playwright/test';

export class NavBar {
  private readonly root: Locator;

  // Scoped to the nav's root element, not the whole page.
  constructor(page: Page) {
    this.root = page.getByRole('navigation', { name: 'Main' });
  }

  async goToSection(name: string) {
    await this.root.getByRole('link', { name }).click();
  }

  async openUserMenu() {
    await this.root.getByRole('button', { name: 'Account' }).click();
  }
}
~~~

~~~ts
// support/data.ts
// A helper that encapsulates real logic — a unique address per run — not a
// one-line wrapper around a Playwright call.
export function uniqueEmail(prefix = 'user'): string {
  const stamp = Date.now().toString(36);
  return `${prefix}+${stamp}@example.com`;
}
~~~

~~~ts
// tests/navigation.spec.ts
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';
import { NavBar } from '../components/nav-bar.component';
import { uniqueEmail } from '../support/data';

test('the nav bar reaches the reports section', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/');
  const nav = new NavBar(page);

  await nav.goToSection('Reports');

  await expect(page.getByRole('heading', { name: 'Reports' })).toBeVisible();
  console.log('would register', uniqueEmail('qa')); // e.g. qa+lz9f3@example.com
});
~~~

The `NavBar` resolves everything inside its root, so it behaves identically on every page and would not clash with a second navigation region. The `uniqueEmail` helper earns its place because it holds real logic several tests would otherwise duplicate — unlike a wrapper around `click()`, which would only hide an API the whole team already knows.$L$, $L$- Duplicating the same widget's locators inside every page object instead of a single component object
- Scoping a "component" to the whole page, so two instances collide under strict mode
- Wrapping single Playwright calls in helpers, hiding a familiar API behind an unfamiliar one
- Extracting an abstraction after the first occurrence, then reshaping it painfully when the second and third turn out different$L$, $L$On a delivery team, mirror the application's own component structure in your test support. If the app has a shared `DatePicker` React component used on ten screens, one `DatePicker` component object serves all ten tests, and when the widget changes you fix it once. Keep a `components/` folder distinct from `pages/`, and keep helpers boring: a helper that anyone can understand at a glance and that holds logic — not a renamed `click` — is one people will actually reuse rather than reinvent.$L$, $L$Identify a UI element that repeats across several screens in an app you know (a nav bar, a modal, a table row). Write a component object scoped to its root with two behaviour methods, and use it in two specs on different pages. Deliverable: the component file and the two specs, plus one example of a helper you deliberately did NOT write because it would only have wrapped a single call.$L$, $L$You have written a flow twice and see it coming a third time. The rule of three says wait; the urge to stay DRY says extract now. What is the actual risk of extracting after only two examples, and why might a little duplication be the cheaper bet?$L$, $L$What makes a component object reusable across different pages? (Answer: it is scoped to a root locator passed in or resolved in its constructor, so all its internal locators resolve within that root rather than against the whole page)$L$, array[$L$I can write a component object scoped to a root locator$L$, $L$I can tell a helper worth writing from a one-line wrapper$L$, $L$I apply the rule of three before extracting an abstraction$L$]
