select m.id, 5, $L$Project Structure, Config & Tags$L$, $L$18 minute read$L$, $L$A framework is also its folders, its naming, and its knobs. This lesson covers a sane project layout, environment configuration that lets one suite run anywhere, and tags that let you slice the suite into a fast smoke run and a full regression pass.$L$, array[$L$Lay out a project so tests, pages, components and support are findable$L$, $L$Drive environment differences through config, not edits to tests$L$, $L$Use tags to carve smoke and regression slices out of one suite$L$], $L$## A layout people can navigate
There is no single correct tree, but a good one makes "where does this go?" and "does this already exist?" answerable at a glance. A common, effective shape:

~~~text
tests/            # the specs — the scenarios themselves
pages/            # page objects
components/       # component objects (shared widgets)
support/          # fixtures, helpers, test data
playwright.config.ts
~~~

The principle matters more than the exact names: **separate the scenarios (tests) from the machinery (pages, components, support).** Some teams group by feature instead — `checkout/` containing its own specs and pages — which scales better on large products. Pick one convention and hold the line; the cost of a mixed, half-migrated structure is worse than either choice made consistently.

## Naming that reads
Consistent naming removes a whole class of small frictions:

- Specs: `login.spec.ts`, `checkout.spec.ts` — named for the behaviour under test.
- Page objects: `login.page.ts` exporting `LoginPage`.
- Components: `nav-bar.component.ts`.
- Test titles: a sentence describing the outcome — `test('rejects a wrong password with a clear message')` — not `test('test login 2')`.

## Environment config, not edited tests
One suite should run against localhost, a review app, staging and (read-only) production without touching a spec. Drive every environment difference through config and environment variables:

- `baseURL` from `process.env.BASE_URL`.
- Credentials and API keys from the environment, never hardcoded or committed.
- Per-environment values (a test tenant, a feature flag) resolved in config, not sprinkled through tests.

If running against a different environment means editing tests, the configuration is in the wrong place.

## Tags: one suite, many slices
You rarely want to run everything every time. **Tags** let you label tests and select subsets. Put a tag in the title or use the `tag` option, then filter with `--grep`:

- `@smoke` — a handful of critical-path tests that must pass on every commit; fast, run on every push.
- `@regression` — the full suite; run before release or nightly.

This is a direct expression of risk and feedback speed: smoke gives you a quick, trustworthy signal that the core still works, regression gives you depth when you can afford the minutes. A green smoke run is not a green regression run — be clear which evidence a given pipeline stage is actually providing.

## Key takeaway
Structure, naming, environment config and tags are the unglamorous half of a framework that decide whether people can find things, run one suite everywhere, and get the right depth of feedback at the right time — pick clear conventions, drive environments through config, and use `@smoke`/`@regression` tags to match feedback speed to risk.$L$, $L$Tagging tests for smoke and regression, and running each slice. Tags are just markers in the title (or the `tag` option) that `--grep` can filter:

~~~ts
// tests/checkout.spec.ts
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('completes a card payment @smoke @regression', async ({ page }) => {
  // critical path — part of both the fast smoke run and full regression
  await page.goto('/checkout');
  await expect(page.getByRole('heading', { name: 'Checkout' })).toBeVisible();
});

test('shows tax breakdown for EU customers @regression', async ({ page }) => {
  // important but not critical-path — regression only
  await page.goto('/checkout?region=eu');
  await expect(page.getByText('VAT')).toBeVisible();
});
~~~

Run just the smoke slice on every push, and the whole thing before release:

~~~bash
# Fast feedback on every commit — critical path only
npx playwright test --grep @smoke

# Full depth before a release
npx playwright test --grep @regression
~~~

Drive the target environment from config so the same specs run anywhere:

~~~ts
// playwright.config.ts (excerpt)
import { defineConfig } from '@playwright/test';

export default defineConfig({
  testDir: './tests',
  use: {
    baseURL: process.env.BASE_URL ?? 'http://localhost:3000',
  },
});
~~~

Now `BASE_URL=https://staging.example.com npx playwright test --grep @smoke` runs the smoke slice against staging with no edit to any spec. The tags express *how much* to run; `baseURL` expresses *where*. Neither concern leaks into the tests themselves.$L$, $L$- Mixing specs, page objects and helpers in one flat folder so nobody can tell scenarios from machinery
- Hardcoding a staging URL or a password in a test, forcing edits to switch environments and leaking secrets into git
- Test titles like `test('test 1')` that tell a reader nothing about what broke when they fail
- Tagging so much as `@smoke` that the smoke run stops being fast, defeating its purpose$L$, $L$On a delivery team, wire the tags straight into the pipeline: `@smoke` runs on every pull request and must be quick — a few minutes at most — so it gates merges without slowing anyone down; `@regression` runs nightly or before a release when depth matters more than speed. Keep the smoke set deliberately small and ruthlessly curated; the day it creeps to twenty minutes, people start bypassing it, and a smoke suite nobody waits for protects nothing. Store all environment specifics — URLs, credentials, tenant ids — in CI secrets and environment variables so the same committed config runs identically for every engineer and every stage.$L$, $L$Organise a small suite into `tests/`, `pages/`, `components/` and `support/`, tag two or three critical tests `@smoke` and the rest `@regression`, and set `baseURL` from an environment variable. Deliverable: the folder tree, the config excerpt, and the two commands that run the smoke slice locally and against a second environment.$L$, $L$Your `@smoke` run has quietly grown to fifteen minutes. It still passes, so is anything wrong? What is the tag actually for, and what have you lost as it grew?$L$, $L$What is the purpose of a @smoke tag versus a @regression tag? (Answer: @smoke marks a small, fast set of critical-path tests run on every commit for quick confidence; @regression marks the full, deeper suite run before release or nightly — the split matches feedback speed to risk)$L$, array[$L$I can lay out a project so scenarios and machinery are separable and findable$L$, $L$I can drive environment differences through config rather than editing tests$L$, $L$I can tag tests into smoke and regression slices and run each$L$]
