insert into public.lessons (module_id, lesson_number, title, estimated_time, lesson_overview, learning_objectives, lesson_notes, worked_example, common_mistakes, real_world_tip, exercise, reflection_question, knowledge_check, completion_checklist)
select m.id, 6, $L$Avoiding Over-Engineering$L$, $L$18 minute read$L$, $L$Every pattern in this module can be overdone. This closing lesson is about judgement: the real cost of premature abstraction, why the "correct" architecture is a myth, and how to keep a framework serving maintainability and trust rather than becoming a monument to cleverness.$L$, array[$L$Name the real, ongoing costs of premature and excessive abstraction$L$, $L$Judge a framework by maintainability and trust, not elegance$L$, $L$Apply pragmatic tests to decide whether an abstraction earns its place$L$], $L$## The seductive failure mode
Over-engineering rarely comes from carelessness; it comes from care. An engineer who has felt the pain of an unstructured suite over-corrects, building base classes, abstract page objects, custom wrappers around Playwright's API and a configuration system for problems the team does not have. Each layer felt responsible at the time. Together they produce a framework that is harder to work in than the mess it replaced.

## The real cost of premature abstraction
Abstraction is not free, and the bill arrives continuously:

- **Cognitive load.** Every layer is something a reader must hold in their head to follow a test. A five-line flow spread across four files is not "cleaner" — it is a five-line flow you now need four files to read.
- **The wrong abstraction.** Extracted too early, from one or two examples, an abstraction encodes a guess about what varies. When reality differs, people bend their tests to fit it, and a wrong abstraction is more expensive to live with than duplication — you have to understand it *and* work around it.
- **Onboarding tax.** A newcomer must learn your bespoke framework before they can write a test, on top of Playwright itself. Custom cleverness has a recurring cost paid by everyone who joins.
- **Hidden behaviour.** Wrappers around the framework hide what Playwright is actually doing, so when something breaks, the familiar documentation no longer maps to your code.

Duplication is a cost you can see and delete later. The wrong abstraction is a cost baked into the shape of everything built on it.

## There is no 'correct' architecture
This module has offered patterns, not laws. Page objects, fixtures, components and tags are tools, and the right amount of each depends on your suite's size, your team, your product's shape and how fast it changes. A five-spec suite that stays flat is well engineered. A thousand-spec suite with disciplined page objects and fixtures is well engineered. The same architecture applied to the wrong one of those is over- or under-engineering. Anyone selling you a single universally correct framework is selling elegance, not maintainability.

## The questions that keep you honest
Before adding a layer, ask:

- **What pain does this remove that I feel today?** Not might feel one day — today.
- **Does this make a test easier or harder to read?** If a reviewer now opens more files to understand a flow, be suspicious.
- **Rule of three:** do I have three real examples, or am I guessing from one?
- **Could a new engineer follow this without a tour?** If it needs explaining, its cost is higher than it looks.

## Architecture serves trust
Come back to why the suite exists. Automation earns its place by giving the team timely, trustworthy evidence about product quality and risk. A framework is good exactly to the degree that it helps the suite do that — by making tests cheap to change, easy to read, and quick to run at the right depth. Elegance that does not serve those ends is decoration, and decoration in a test framework is pure cost. A green suite the team does not trust, or cannot afford to maintain, has failed no matter how beautiful its class hierarchy.

## Key takeaway
Over-engineering is a failure of care, not carelessness: premature abstraction charges cognitive load, wrong-shape lock-in and an onboarding tax forever, while duplication is a visible cost you can remove later. There is no single correct architecture — judge every layer by whether it makes the suite more maintainable and more trustworthy, and delete anything that only makes it more elegant.$L$, $L$The same requirement — click a named button — solved with a needless abstraction and then without it. First, the over-engineered version:

~~~ts
// support/actions.ts — a wrapper layer around Playwright's own API
import { Page } from '@playwright/test';

export class ActionHelper {
  constructor(private page: Page) {}
  async clickButton(name: string) {
    await this.page.getByRole('button', { name }).click();
  }
  async fillField(label: string, value: string) {
    await this.page.getByLabel(label).fill(value);
  }
}

// test
const actions = new ActionHelper(page);
await actions.fillField('Email', 'user@example.com');
await actions.clickButton('Sign in');
~~~

This adds a class, a file and a vocabulary the team must learn, purely to rename `getByRole(...).click()` — an API everyone already knows. It hides Playwright's behaviour behind a thinner, less documented one, and buys nothing: no duplication removed, no intent clarified. When `fillField` mishandles a field type, the fix is in your wrapper, and the Playwright docs no longer describe your code.

The version without the abstraction is shorter, clearer and needs no onboarding:

~~~ts
// test — uses the framework everyone already knows
await page.getByLabel('Email').fill('user@example.com');
await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Sign in' }).click();
~~~

Contrast this with a page object's `login()` from Lesson 2, which *does* earn its place: it removes real duplication and names a user-recognisable behaviour. The difference is not "abstraction good or bad" — it is whether the abstraction buys one of Lesson 1's three benefits. `login()` does; `clickButton()` does not.$L$, $L$- Wrapping Playwright's API in a bespoke layer that renames familiar methods and buys nothing
- Building base classes and abstract page objects for a suite that has not yet felt the pain they solve
- Defending an abstraction because it is elegant or DRY rather than because it makes tests easier to change or read
- Keeping a wrong-shaped abstraction alive and bending tests around it, when deleting it and tolerating duplication would be cheaper$L$, $L$On a delivery team, make "delete the abstraction" a normal, low-drama move. Frameworks accrete cleverness over time; every quarter or so, look for layers that no longer earn their place — a wrapper used twice, a base class one page extends, a config option nobody sets — and remove them. The test of a healthy framework is not how much it contains but how easily a new engineer writes their first genuinely useful test. If that takes longer than an afternoon, your architecture is working against the very trust and maintainability it was meant to provide.$L$, $L$Review a test framework (yours or a sample) and find one abstraction you believe is not earning its place. Write a short justification against the four honest questions in this lesson, then remove it and inline its callers. Deliverable: a before/after of one affected spec and a paragraph on whether the suite is now easier or harder to read — and be willing to conclude the abstraction should stay.$L$, $L$You inherit a framework with a beautiful, elegant class hierarchy that the team is afraid to change and slow to onboard into. By the standard that architecture serves maintainability and trust, is it well engineered? What would you measure to decide?$L$, $L$Why is a wrong abstraction often more expensive than duplication? (Answer: duplication is a visible cost you can find and delete later, whereas a wrong abstraction is baked into the shape of everything built on it — people must understand it and work around it, and unpicking it is far harder than removing repeated code)$L$, array[$L$I can name the ongoing costs of premature and excessive abstraction$L$, $L$I judge a framework by maintainability and trust rather than elegance$L$, $L$I can decide, and justify, whether a given abstraction earns its place$L$]
